WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:01.909
Luca, welcome back!

00:00:01.909 --> 00:00:05.240
Thanks. It's good to be back here.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.360
So, how does it feel to be back?

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:17.460
It feels a bit like when I go back to Italy.
And everything is good, and people are great...

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:18.460
The espresso's better.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.280
The espresso's good, yes. But things are a
bit complicated.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.060
Of course

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.420
Yes, we do complexity here.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:29.300
Right.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.480
We have known each other for the longest time,
right? 

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.660
You were an undergraduate when I first met you in Italy.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.160
Yes.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.860
I spent a sabbatical in Rome and there you
were. I was really amazed to meet you. 

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.880
I have a good eye.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:55.010
Yes, I was a senior in college. And you knew
my advisor, who I guess was your postdoc 

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.310
at San Diego the year before. 

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.760
I had a really
great time doing a somewhat research-oriented senior thesis. 

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.580
And I didn't get any offer
of a real job after I graduated.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.960
So I started doing grad school.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:11.460
Great.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.060
That seems to have worked out pretty well.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.499
At some point, you started working at IBM.

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:26.159
Yes. Pilu Crescenzi, my advisor, he also knew
Madhu Sudan, who was at IBM at that point.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:32.179
And in my second year of grad school, as was
pretty common for students in my program,

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:37.209
I was looking for somewhere to go to and spend
a period abroad. So I wrote to Madhu to say that

00:01:37.209 --> 00:01:45.009
I would like to come to IBM and visit,
and the Italian government would pay for the visit.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.880
And Madhu was happy. This was off-season,
so not in summer but in the fall, where they

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.039
don't have a lot of students in general. And
they were always happy to have someone new.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:02.040
And so I went. And very luckily, just a few
weeks before going

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.080
I was reading this paper on PCP and hardness of approximation

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.480
that Mihir Bellare and Oded Goldreich and Madhu

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:15.150
had just put online. It was a 120-page paper.
And it was the first time that a lot of those

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:20.480
new ideas on how to go beyond the original
PCP construction and get stronger results

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.959
were written down with all the details. And
I'd been thinking about one section where

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:32.959
they show how you do reductions from PCP to
optimization problems with gadgets.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.980
They had a very abstract way of thinking about that.
And I was thinking that it should be possible

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.360
to search for an optimal gadget automatically.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.440
Right. So this is where this idea came from.
Okay, right. It originated in Italy. (Laughs.)

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:53.600
Indeed. So I flew to New York with Alitalia.
And they lost my bags, with all my stuff.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:00.000
And I then had to get a car to get to the
IBM lab, which was a bit outside of New York City. 

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.969
But I was not 25, so I couldn't rent
a car. And so my first day there, with no

00:03:05.969 --> 00:03:11.269
clothes and no house and no car, but, still,
I had this idea. 

00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:18.240
And I told Madhu, and he immediately got it. 

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.980
And then the following  day

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.560
he basically reexplained the idea to me, but much, much better.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:25.230
Wow.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.940
Then we talked to Greg Sorkin and David Williamson

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.620
who showed how it could be extended to other problems. 

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.880
So in a few days, we had a pretty
nice piece of work.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.180
Great. You kept working with Madhu after that
- or not?

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.079
We stayed in touch. Just before I left, we
were starting to work on a classification

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:48.939
of constraint-satisfaction problems.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:49.580
I see.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.620
 Yes, of course. 

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:01.810
Then I ran out of my graduate fellowship.
Which, the way it was working in Italy at

00:04:01.889 --> 00:04:06.769
that point (or not working) was that the fellowship
would run out one year before you would actually

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:12.780
graduate. Or sort of the time it took to get
together the graduation bureaucracy took a

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:19.040
year after that. So I spent that gap-year
in Geneva. And I started to work on pseudorandomness

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:19.790
and extractors.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:25.350
Yes, that was also very lucky.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:32.080
So finally, I got officially my PhD and I
was going to spend a year again in New York

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:39.080
at IBM working with Madhu. But at that point,
Madhu got offered to move to MIT and so he

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:46.140
told me, would you like to come to MIT instead?
And I said, well, I would very much like to

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.780
come to MIT instead. And so off I went.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:55.230
Then you went to Columbia after that year.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:59.640
Yes - I was originally thinking that maybe
after that year abroad, I would go back to

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:06.640
Italy. But that year at MIT was life-changing:
There were a lot of great people there. Oded

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:11.760
Goldreich was there on sabbatical, and he
had a big impact on me. The students there,

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:20.240
they were... Amit Sahai was there, Salil Vadan,
Danny Lewin, Adam Klivans, Yevgeny Dodis,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.580
Tal Malkin, Daniele Micciancio, Anna Lysyanskaya...

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:32.460
It was a great group. And in the spring semester,
we did this reading group to try to understand

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.460
the work of Avi Wigderson and Russell Impagliazzo
that had just come out, where they were showing

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:42.090
that P equals PPP under plausible assumptions.
And we actually spent like three months on

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:47.130
that, because that paper was building upon
five or six papers from the previous ten years.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:52.430
We decided that we would really like to understand
the whole sequence - with no particular goal

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:57.430
in mind, because it seemed that the work of
Russell and Avi was the final word on the

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:05.120
matter. And, instead, that reading group led
to a lot of new work. So Adam Klivans had

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:12.120
this idea of applying the same stuff non-deterministically.
So, independently with Dieter van Melkebeek,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:18.970
he proved that graph non-isomorphism is in
NP, under complexity assumptions. And then

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:26.470
I did this work on extractors and pseudorandom
generators that seems to be the best thing I've done.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:28.660
Yes, well, we will be the judge, okay. (Laughs.)

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:36.470
Salil had some improvement on that that Omer
Reingold and Ran Raz also had independently.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:40.270
So they started working together.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.940
So Omer
and Salil started working together because of that.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.700
Ran was also in that group?

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:54.700
No, Omer and Ran were in Israel. I told
Avi about the result, and Avi told Ran, and

00:06:55.100 --> 00:07:00.390
then Ran asked for the paper, and then immediately
saw this improvement, and Salil also. And

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:05.940
that was the beginning of Omer and Salil working
together.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:13.740
This had so much impact that even we at Berkeley
heard about it, and decided to hire you.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:21.560
Yes. And at that point I had pretty much never
been to California. And I was enjoying life

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:28.560
in New York quite a bit. But everybody was
telling me that they wouldn't speak to me

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.440
again if I didn't accept the Berkeley offer.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.080
Really? I thought it was only us who told
you that.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:43.410
Sure. (Laughs.) But everybody else was very
adamant that I had to take this job.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:50.950
Amazing. That's good to know. Great, so, that's how we became colleagues.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.740
What I remember most fondly, is your

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.240
let's call it intellectual leadership

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:05.480
That whenever there was something interesting

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:09.620
you took the chalk, or took the
marker

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:13.440
and started explaining to us at Theory Lunch.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.260
I learned so many things from you.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.160
It was here that you started your blog - am I right?

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:25.160
Yes. This was in 2006. It was the first year
that Andy Yao had started his institute at

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:31.970
Tsinghua University in Beijing and one of
my students, Hoeteck Wee was spending a whole

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:37.180
year there. He was one of three American students
who were there - two from Berkeley, one from

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:43.719
MIT. So it was spring break of 2006; it was
in the middle of this year. And I went to

00:08:43.719 --> 00:08:50.719
spend a week, giving a couple of talks to
the students there in Beijing - talking to

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:57.810
them and also seeing China for the first time.
So this before at least I had Facebook.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:03.260
It was not before Facebook, but before your
Facebook.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:10.260
Before I did, right. I'm a late adopter. So
I wanted someplace where I could send friends

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.640
pictures and stories of what was going on.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.590
So much to say, yes, right.

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:20.320
So I started this blog, and I sent the link
to a couple of people. By the end of the week,

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.840
lots of people were reading it. So when I
came back, I thought, maybe I'll keep it running

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:30.300
for a few more weeks and write about some
technical stuff. And then eight years later,

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.960
it's still going.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:37.300
I was a great fan; I hope to keep reading
it here

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.260
Additive combinatorics?

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.820
Yeah, that was a time that it seemed that

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:49.980
there was this work of Ben Green and Terry Tao

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.760
where they proved that the primes have
arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:10:02.480
And there was a notion of pseudorandomness
in that paper. They already knew that

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:10.940
dense sets of integers have arbitrary long arithmetic
progressions. And they knew that

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:17.820
the integers that in some technical sense are almost prime, are pseudorandom in a very strong sense. And

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.720
that the primes were dense inside these almost-primes.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.720
And so they had this intuition that

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:32.380
if dense sets of integers have this property, sets that are dense in a pseudorandom sense will

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:38.880
also have this property. So they made it work.
And what I thought would be really interesting

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.779
was to see if there was a way to understand
what they were doing in the way we think about

00:10:44.779 --> 00:10:51.279
pseudorandomness. Which there was, and that
led to a few interesting pieces of work.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.320
And what came next?

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:59.340
You worked more on approximability
and PCP?

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:02.100
You worked on the Unique Games? 

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:08.540
Yes. In complexity theory, the two areas I
keep going back to are hardness of approximation

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:14.120
and approximation algorithms, and pseudorandomness
and average-case complexity. And it was at

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:18.180
that point that it seemed that these areas
and additive combinatorics had some connection

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:24.110
that I wanted to explore. And what I worked
on next was motivated by the fact that it

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:31.110
seemed that random walks in graphs, and expanded
graphs, and properties of expanders and so

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:38.160
on, seemed to also come up a lot in various
areas of complexity theory.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.740
So I thought I would spend maybe a few months learning about spectral graph theory from the beginning,

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:50.200
and have those techniques a bit more clear
in my mind. Well, that was five years ago,

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:58.540
and I'm still working on it, because I found
that it was so much... there are so many open questions

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:04.060
and it's so connected to new work
on approximation algorithms, or even exact

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:07.850
algorithms - like the work on max flow using
electrical networks.

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:17.660
Yes, that's right. And so much that you are
going to be having this semester program starting

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:18.710
in a few days.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:22.730
Yes. Next week, or in a couple of weeks, we'll
be having the program on spectral graph theory,

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:29.730
where all the people who we were hoping would
come came. And it promises to be really exciting.

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:38.040
It's coming at the right time: There are already
a lot of things that people have been developing,

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:45.040
and it's a good place to share them. But it
also seems that there is a tipping point,

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:49.420
and something much bigger is going to happen.
And maybe the Institute...

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:54.630
We want to be the locus, the place for tipping
points. That's what the Simons Institute,

00:12:54.630 --> 00:13:03.990
as you know, is for. Yes, right... How are
you looking at your role at the Institute?

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:11.020
You know, when I interviewed here at Berkeley,
it was a bit different from the way interviews

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:17.279
usually go. Because there everybody tells
you what the place is like, and what your

00:13:17.279 --> 00:13:21.460
work is going to be like in this place. But
when I interviewed here, people were asking me...

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:22.260
You define it.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.900
"What are you going to do with this Senior
Scientist job?" And I'm still not so sure.

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:31.600
But I think that really, ever since that experience at MIT

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.980
Let me tell you: Two years later, I have no
idea. (Laughs.)

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:42.200
Ever since that experience at MIT, with that
reading group, where we were just interested

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:47.990
in one thing and all kinds of things came
out of it, I've really been interested in

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:52.380
exploring new things I don't know much about.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:59.380
Serendipity. Yes. I love that. This sounds
like a great plan.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:05.240
It seems here, I can do that as a job.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.550
As a job, and very intensely. Yes, right.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:12.050
When new things are coming up, and try to
connect with people who might be good organizers

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:12.939
for new programs...

